1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,466
Two years ago, America's biggest power grid paid

2
00:00:04,466 --> 00:00:08,749
twenty-nine dollars to keep one megawatt on standby for a day.

3
00:00:09,049 --> 00:00:11,517
This year: three hundred and twenty-nine.

4
00:00:11,817 --> 00:00:16,259
More than ten times as much — mainly because of data centers.

5
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:21,025
And sixty-seven million people pay for it, in their electric bills.

6
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:26,038
But the power industry's own research lab found that until recently,

7
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:29,261
data centers were making those bills cheaper.

8
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:30,915
Both are true.

9
00:00:31,465 --> 00:00:32,631
So what flipped?

10
00:00:33,481 --> 00:00:37,648
Open your electric bill and it looks like one number. It isn't.

11
00:00:37,948 --> 00:00:40,994
Some of it pays for the power itself. Some pays

12
00:00:40,994 --> 00:00:45,074
for the wires that carry it. And one slice pays for something

13
00:00:45,074 --> 00:00:47,804
most people have never heard of: capacity.

14
00:00:48,104 --> 00:00:52,428
Capacity is the price of being ready. A grid can't just have

15
00:00:52,428 --> 00:00:55,645
enough power on an average day. It needs enough

16
00:00:55,645 --> 00:00:58,768
on the hottest afternoon of the year, plus a cushion.

17
00:00:59,068 --> 00:01:03,610
So every year, the grid buys that readiness in advance, at an auction.

18
00:01:03,910 --> 00:01:08,545
In thirteen states and Washington, DC, the grid operator

19
00:01:08,545 --> 00:01:12,121
is PJM. The same grid from the start of this video.

20
00:01:12,421 --> 00:01:16,046
Here's how the auction works. PJM first calculates

21
00:01:16,046 --> 00:01:21,130
how many megawatts it will need on the hottest days, cushion included.

22
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:25,570
Power plants bid, each naming the lowest price it will accept.

23
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:29,994
PJM lines the bids up from cheapest to most expensive,

24
00:01:29,994 --> 00:01:33,518
and buys from the bottom until it has enough.

25
00:01:33,968 --> 00:01:38,214
And here's the part that matters. Every plant that wins is paid

26
00:01:38,214 --> 00:01:42,562
the price of the last one needed. One price, for everyone.

27
00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:46,293
So when demand grows and supply is tight, the last

28
00:01:46,293 --> 00:01:49,852
plant costs more. And everyone gets paid more.

29
00:01:50,152 --> 00:01:53,133
The utilities that buy this power for customers

30
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:56,065
pay that price. It ends up in your bill.

31
00:01:56,365 --> 00:02:00,969
Now look at that price, in dollars per megawatt, per day. Two years

32
00:02:00,969 --> 00:02:04,681
ago: twenty-nine. Then two hundred and seventy.

33
00:02:04,681 --> 00:02:09,330
Then three hundred and twenty-nine. Then three hundred and thirty-three.

34
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,735
The most recent auction, in July,

35
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:14,423
cleared at three hundred and twenty-five.

36
00:02:14,723 --> 00:02:19,943
The last three all hit a price cap. PJM's own simulation says that without

37
00:02:19,943 --> 00:02:24,791
the cap, the latest would have cleared above five hundred and fifty.

38
00:02:25,141 --> 00:02:28,707
So the price of readiness went up more than eleven times

39
00:02:28,707 --> 00:02:31,172
in two years, and then hit a ceiling.

40
00:02:31,472 --> 00:02:35,525
Keep it in proportion, though. By the market monitor's count,

41
00:02:35,525 --> 00:02:39,782
capacity was about seventeen percent of the wholesale cost of power

42
00:02:39,782 --> 00:02:44,376
in early twenty twenty-six, up from about eight percent a year earlier.

43
00:02:45,226 --> 00:02:48,701
Now the surprising part. Nationally, for years,

44
00:02:48,701 --> 00:02:51,731
data centers appear to have done the opposite.

45
00:02:52,031 --> 00:02:57,445
In June, researchers at EPRI, the Electric Power Research Institute,

46
00:02:57,445 --> 00:03:02,852
posted a paper titled: Have Data Centers Raised Your Electric Bill?

47
00:03:03,152 --> 00:03:08,399
Quick note on who they are. EPRI is the electric power industry's

48
00:03:08,399 --> 00:03:14,551
research institute, funded by its members, mostly electric utilities.

49
00:03:14,551 --> 00:03:18,033
That doesn't make the numbers wrong. But you should know it.

50
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:22,888
What did they find? From twenty nineteen to twenty twenty-four,

51
00:03:22,888 --> 00:03:25,982
the average American household lived in a state

52
00:03:25,982 --> 00:03:30,363
where data center capacity grew a hundred and sixty percent.

53
00:03:30,363 --> 00:03:34,242
They estimate that growth made residential electricity rates

54
00:03:34,242 --> 00:03:38,207
about six percent lower than they would otherwise have been.

55
00:03:38,507 --> 00:03:42,935
Put another way: each time a state's data center capacity doubled,

56
00:03:42,935 --> 00:03:48,322
residential rates fell by about three and a half percent. They're clear it's

57
00:03:48,322 --> 00:03:52,917
an estimate, with a wide range, from about one and a half percent to twelve.

58
00:03:53,217 --> 00:03:57,455
Why would more demand lower the price? Because most of a grid's

59
00:03:57,455 --> 00:04:00,958
cost is fixed: the power lines, the substations.

60
00:04:00,958 --> 00:04:05,868
When more electricity flows through the same wires, each unit carries a smaller

61
00:04:05,868 --> 00:04:09,855
share. And new power plants can cost less than the old ones.

62
00:04:10,155 --> 00:04:14,310
Columbia University's Center on Global Energy Policy said

63
00:04:14,310 --> 00:04:19,038
in June that recent price increases can't be pinned on any single

64
00:04:19,038 --> 00:04:23,773
factor, and that big new loads have not been a principal driver.

65
00:04:24,073 --> 00:04:28,275
But the discount comes with a condition. As one energy economist

66
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:32,941
put it, the states where prices fell already had room on their grids.

67
00:04:33,241 --> 00:04:36,716
And blaming data centers for every price increase would

68
00:04:36,716 --> 00:04:41,383
be wrong too. From twenty twenty-one to twenty twenty-four,

69
00:04:41,383 --> 00:04:45,664
California's electricity prices rose nearly forty percent,

70
00:04:45,664 --> 00:04:49,660
which the same paper says was largely wildfire costs.

71
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:54,038
So if data centers were pushing prices down, how did one

72
00:04:54,038 --> 00:04:58,462
price jump more than nine times, from one auction to the next?

73
00:04:59,312 --> 00:05:01,711
The answer is in one word: room.

74
00:05:02,011 --> 00:05:05,651
Spreading fixed costs only works while the grid has spare

75
00:05:05,651 --> 00:05:09,791
capacity. When it's full, a new customer doesn't share the cost

76
00:05:09,791 --> 00:05:13,782
of the existing system. A new customer needs a new one.

77
00:05:14,082 --> 00:05:18,367
Look at where PJM's growth is coming from. For the auction

78
00:05:18,367 --> 00:05:21,886
covering twenty twenty-seven and twenty twenty-eight,

79
00:05:21,886 --> 00:05:27,415
its forecast peak demand rose by about fifty-two hundred and fifty megawatts.

80
00:05:27,415 --> 00:05:30,677
Nearly fifty-one hundred of that was data centers.

81
00:05:30,977 --> 00:05:35,396
Supply didn't keep up. That auction added only seven hundred

82
00:05:35,396 --> 00:05:39,319
and seventy-four megawatts of new generation and upgrades.

83
00:05:39,619 --> 00:05:44,897
And for the first time, the whole region's total fell short of PJM's own

84
00:05:44,897 --> 00:05:50,329
reliability target, by sixty-six hundred and twenty-three megawatts.

85
00:05:50,329 --> 00:05:55,489
PJM says that doesn't mean the lights go out. It means thinner reserves.

86
00:05:55,789 --> 00:05:59,062
More demand, less new supply, and the last plant

87
00:05:59,062 --> 00:06:03,005
needed sets the price for everyone. That's the jump.

88
00:06:03,455 --> 00:06:09,278
PJM has an independent watchdog, the market monitor. In June twenty

89
00:06:09,278 --> 00:06:14,083
twenty-five, it wrote that data center load growth is, in its words,

90
00:06:14,083 --> 00:06:19,109
the primary reason for recent and expected capacity market conditions.

91
00:06:19,409 --> 00:06:24,095
Its estimate for the twenty twenty-five, twenty twenty-six auction:

92
00:06:24,095 --> 00:06:28,574
data center load alone raised that auction's total by about nine

93
00:06:28,574 --> 00:06:33,341
point three billion dollars, or a hundred and seventy-four percent.

94
00:06:33,641 --> 00:06:38,172
In July, it added up the last four auctions: about twenty-nine

95
00:06:38,172 --> 00:06:42,433
point four billion dollars tied to data center load.

96
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:47,526
Forty-six percent of all capacity charges across those four auctions.

97
00:06:47,826 --> 00:06:51,672
Nine billion. Twenty-nine billion. Forty-six percent.

98
00:06:51,672 --> 00:06:56,539
Those are the monitor's estimates, and they measure charges in the auction,

99
00:06:56,539 --> 00:07:01,151
not what lands on each household's bill. But the direction is clear.

100
00:07:01,701 --> 00:07:06,436
And here's what makes it strange. A lot of that demand isn't here yet.

101
00:07:06,736 --> 00:07:11,272
The auction doesn't run on how much power data centers use today.

102
00:07:11,272 --> 00:07:16,381
It runs on a forecast. In January, the monitor found that about six

103
00:07:16,381 --> 00:07:21,355
point two billion of the six and a half billion dollars it tied to data

104
00:07:21,355 --> 00:07:26,272
centers in the December auction came from data centers not yet built.

105
00:07:26,572 --> 00:07:31,322
It called the uncertainty in those forecasts unique and unprecedented.

106
00:07:31,622 --> 00:07:35,904
One reason: developers often don't tell a utility where else they've

107
00:07:35,904 --> 00:07:40,539
asked for power. According to the Associated Press, PJM says

108
00:07:40,539 --> 00:07:45,869
that means a single project can inflate more than one utility's forecast.

109
00:07:46,219 --> 00:07:49,464
That means part of today's price rests on buildings

110
00:07:49,464 --> 00:07:53,991
that don't exist yet. Some will be built. Some may not.

111
00:07:54,841 --> 00:07:56,065
So who pays?

112
00:07:56,365 --> 00:07:59,946
The market monitor describes the problem plainly.

113
00:07:59,946 --> 00:08:03,349
It wants data centers to pay for their own capacity,

114
00:08:03,349 --> 00:08:06,644
and not impose capacity costs on other customers.

115
00:08:06,944 --> 00:08:10,317
That tells you how it works today: the cost is shared.

116
00:08:10,617 --> 00:08:14,767
How much does that add up to for a household? One estimate,

117
00:08:14,767 --> 00:08:18,860
from the think tank IEEFA, is that the higher capacity

118
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:23,028
prices add about eighteen dollars a month to an average

119
00:08:23,028 --> 00:08:27,643
bill in western Maryland, and about sixteen dollars in Ohio.

120
00:08:27,943 --> 00:08:32,450
Careful with those numbers. They cover the whole jump in capacity

121
00:08:32,450 --> 00:08:36,658
prices, not only the data center share, and they're estimates.

122
00:08:36,958 --> 00:08:41,036
Now the other side. Across the country, residential

123
00:08:41,036 --> 00:08:45,710
electricity prices are up about forty-two percent in five years,

124
00:08:45,710 --> 00:08:49,167
according to federal data cited by PolitiFact.

125
00:08:49,467 --> 00:08:53,982
EPRI's researchers say aging equipment, disaster recovery

126
00:08:53,982 --> 00:08:58,803
and natural gas prices have mattered more than data centers.

127
00:08:59,253 --> 00:09:03,309
So both things are true at once. Nationally, data centers

128
00:09:03,309 --> 00:09:07,086
are not the main reason bills rose. But in a grid

129
00:09:07,086 --> 00:09:11,165
with no room left, they can be the main reason one price did.

130
00:09:12,015 --> 00:09:15,009
The rules are changing, state by state.

131
00:09:15,009 --> 00:09:16,922
Here's what has actually happened.

132
00:09:17,222 --> 00:09:22,076
Oregon passed House Bill thirty-five forty-six. It tells the state's

133
00:09:22,076 --> 00:09:26,781
regulator to create a separate rate class for very large energy users,

134
00:09:26,781 --> 00:09:31,040
with costs assigned to them and protections for everyone else.

135
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:35,713
Texas signed Senate Bill six in June twenty twenty-five.

136
00:09:35,713 --> 00:09:39,318
It makes very large new loads, seventy-five megawatts

137
00:09:39,318 --> 00:09:43,607
and up, contribute to the cost of connecting them to the grid.

138
00:09:43,907 --> 00:09:47,613
In Virginia, regulators approved a separate rate class

139
00:09:47,613 --> 00:09:51,656
for customers using twenty-five megawatts or more, starting

140
00:09:51,656 --> 00:09:56,021
in January twenty twenty-seven. They must sign fourteen-year

141
00:09:56,021 --> 00:09:59,519
contracts with minimum payments, even if they use less.

142
00:09:59,819 --> 00:10:03,883
And the market monitor wants a bigger change: take data centers

143
00:10:03,883 --> 00:10:08,224
out of the shared capacity market and give them their own auction.

144
00:10:08,524 --> 00:10:13,311
None of this changes yesterday's prices. It shapes the next ones.

145
00:10:14,161 --> 00:10:17,282
So, will a data center raise your electric bill?

146
00:10:17,582 --> 00:10:21,621
Not automatically. The real question is two questions.

147
00:10:21,921 --> 00:10:25,466
One: does your grid still have room? If it does, new

148
00:10:25,466 --> 00:10:29,157
demand can spread fixed costs and push prices down.

149
00:10:29,157 --> 00:10:33,050
If it doesn't, the next megawatt gets expensive for everyone.

150
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:38,518
Two: when it isn't cheap, who pays? Right now, a lot of that cost is shared.

151
00:10:38,518 --> 00:10:42,593
The rules being written now will decide whether it stays that way.

152
00:10:43,243 --> 00:10:46,233
Is a data center being built near you — and has

153
00:10:46,233 --> 00:10:49,780
your electric bill changed? Tell me in the comments.

